---
name: zero-arg-all-defaults
description: "V.m() on an object method whose every parameter has a default is a CALL, while V.m is a value"
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# `V.m()` calls; `V.m` does not

`BUGS.md` `v1-interp-zero-arg-call-to-all-defaulted-object-method-returns-a-closure`. An object's
members are fields of its instance, and a field holding a function was only ever *called* when it had
no parameters. So `V.one()` on `def one(a: Int = 3)` returned the closure — printing
`<function(1)>` where every other lane printed `3`.

The asymmetry is the tell: a top-level `def` and a **class** method with the identical signature were
both correct. Only object members took the field path.

```scalascript
object V:
  def one(a: Int = 3): Int = a
  def two(a: Int = 3, b: Int = 4): Int = a * 10 + b
  def none(): Int = 7
  def req(a: Int): Int = a

def plainOne(a: Int = 3): Int = a

println(V.one())
println(V.two())
println(V.none())
println(plainOne())
println(V.one(5))
println(V.req(6))
```

**The parenless form must stay a value, and that is the reason the fix is not where the bug is.**
Measured on three lanes before touching anything, `V.one` without parentheses is a function value
everywhere — INT `<function(1)>`, native `<closure>`, the JVM a lambda. By the time a member reaches
the dispatcher, `V.one` and `V.one()` are the same call with an empty argument list, so deciding
there would have changed both. The empty list is only visible one level up, in the caller, and that
is where the decision is made.

`V.none()` (no parameters at all) and `V.req(6)` (a required parameter, supplied) are the guards on
the two paths that already worked; `plainOne()` guards the top-level `def` that was never affected.
